WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070
- When firms become dominant,

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.630
they can become too big to care.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.550
- [Narrator] That's FTC Chair Lina Kahn

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.890
at The Wall Street Journal's CEO Council.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:08.970
She's answering a question

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.310
about the Taylor Swift
Ticketmaster Fiasco,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.990
a situation that's renewed calls

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.330
to undo its decade-old
merger with Live Nation,

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.860
which has plagued Washington, D.C.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.110
and the music industry for years.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.540
Tickets to Swift's first tour
in five years went on sale

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.690
to Verified Fans and then
to Capital One card holders.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.620
But in both cases,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.410
a glitchy system kept fans
from securing tickets.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.820
- Once they got into the system,

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.070
they'd click on tickets
that would disappear.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.830
They'd get kicked out,
they'd get error messages.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.210
- [Narrator] Which prompted furious fans

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.260
to take to the internet.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.850
- What do you mean something went wrong?

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.590
I'm not doing this today, Ticketmaster.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.600
- [Narrator] And lawmakers
to call for scrutiny.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.800
- There's no other choice.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.710
That's why we are pushing
the Justice Department

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:47.700
to look at this.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.410
- [Narrator] Before tickets
could open to the public,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.660
Ticketmaster canceled the sale.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.630
So how did Ticketmaster reach
the point where lawmakers

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.670
and fans are calling for scrutiny?

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.740
Fan testimonies, congressional hearings,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.933
as well as court documents tell the story.

00:01:01.916 --> 00:01:02.749
(fans cheering)

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:05.100
Now Taylor Swift fans
are suing Ticketmaster

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.190
over claims that the ticketing
giant violated antitrust laws

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.070
and some lawmakers are
even calling it a monopoly.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.230
To understand why, we have to go back.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.420
The timeline starts here in 2009

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.890
when two of the largest companies

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.590
in the live event space
sought to join together.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.347
- Will the combined company
be tempted to divert tickets

00:01:22.347 --> 00:01:25.470
to the resale market at inflated prices

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.690
because there are now no competitors

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.310
to keep this behavior in check?

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.290
- Live Nation CEO Michael Rapino,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.620
and then CEO of Ticketmaster
Irving Azoff pled their case

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.230
in front of the Judiciary
Subcommittee on Antitrust.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.540
- It was 2009, so this is right

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.790
in the thick of the financial crisis.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:45.090
Concert promotion's a really,
really tough margin business

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.190
whereas ticketing, on the other hand,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.560
is a very high-margin business.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.030
- [Narrator] This financial need was one

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.140
of the main talking points

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.750
at the time for why the
two required a merger.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.190
- I have two choices.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.320
I can hope the economy gets better

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.970
or I can seek a more proactive approach

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.760
to protect our employees,
reward our shareholders

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.740
and better service artists and fans.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.670
- [Narrator] Of course, the committee

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.500
also heard the opposing case.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.630
Antitrust law attorney David A. Balto,

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.190
as well as some music promoters,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.990
each testified against the merger.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.060
- From a straightforward
horizontal concern,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.980
this merger is anti-competitive.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.970
- The fear was that this
would create this behemoth

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:21.840
that would kind

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.170
of have a strangle hold
on the music business.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.340
- [Narrator] That's because Ticketmaster

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.320
was already the dominant
ticketing provider

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.290
while Live Nation was the
largest promoter of live shows.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.810
Blending the two would mean
the company would have a hand

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.850
and pocketbook in nearly every step

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.290
of the live event business.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.060
- When you have that type of integration,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.580
it can create certain types
of conflicts of interest.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.947
At the time, there was a concern

00:02:42.947 --> 00:02:46.680
that Ticketmaster might use its position

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.120
to tie those things together,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.580
and to say, well, in order
to have access to this venue,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.950
you actually have to
use our ticketing site.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:53.940
- [Narrator] The Department of Justice

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.260
ultimately allowed the merger

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.120
after coming to an
agreement with the companies

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.370
through what is known as a consent decree.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.840
- Under the settlement, it says

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.580
that Live Nation is not allowed

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.090
to strong arm venues
into using Ticketmaster.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.250
- [Narrator] But almost 10
years later, reports broke

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.320
that the DOJ was preparing legal action

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.700
for that very reason.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.890
- According to this report,
it believes it violated

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.330
a merger settlement

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.290
by trying to coerce concert
venues into using Ticketmaster.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.690
- Without Live Nation
admitting any wrongdoing,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:25.470
a new settlement with the
Department of Justice was reached.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.630
I think a lot of people viewed it

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.820
as sort of a slap on the wrist.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.560
- [Narrator] Then as
the Biden administration

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.030
took office, lawmakers

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.610
like US Representative
Bill Pascrell voiced hope

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.290
for stricter antitrust laws.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.000
In a letter to the admin, Pascrell,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.987
along with several other Democrats said,

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:43.770
"The evidence of strangled
competition is overwhelming"

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.050
and called on the admin
to revisit the merger.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.230
- We've seen these agencies,
the DOJ and the FDC,

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.690
really be encouraged to
take a more critical eye

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.310
toward these big mergers.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:54.630
- [Narrator] As part of a broader effort,

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.540
President Biden signed an
executive order last year aimed

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.850
at promoting competition
in the American economy.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.740
- Let me be very clear,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.430
capitalism without
competition isn't capitalism.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.170
It's exploitation.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.780
- But that fall, as live
events began revitalizing

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.750
from the COVID-19 halt, issues
with Ticketmaster's site

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.160
continued to mount.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.080
Similar problems to Swift's Eras tour

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.000
happened last year with the BTS concert

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.680
and Olivia Rodrigo's too.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.940
Then in December this year,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.620
Ticketmaster apologized again,

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.882
this time for a Bad Bunny
ticket fiasco in Mexico City.

00:04:25.882 --> 00:04:29.900
♪ I remember it all too well ♪

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.470
- [Narrator] In the
current live event market,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.310
it's not uncommon

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.160
for a ticketing site to get
overwhelmed with high volume

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.160
but this reached an entirely
new scale with Taylor Swift.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.780
- For the first day of pre-sales,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.660
they sold over two million
tickets to her show,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.280
which is the most tickets sold

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.280
for any single artist in a single day

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.900
in Ticketmaster history.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.400
- [Narrator] Ticketmaster
sold tickets to all

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.890
but five of her 52 stadium shows.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.300
The ones at the State Farm Stadium

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.210
and the AT&amp;T stadium were
sold through SeatGeek.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.980
That's because of a
business practice known

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.320
as exclusive ticketing contracts.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.330
- In that case, Ticketmaster goes

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.430
to venues and inks an exclusive deal,

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.240
which means anything that
comes through this stadium

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.920
or arena is gonna be sold
through Ticketmaster.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.420
- [Narrator] In an interview with CNBC,

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.270
Liberty CEO and Live
Nation chairman Greg Maffei

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.070
credited the company's stellar services

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.240
for its market share.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.660
- Our competitor, who is the
promoter for Taylor Swift,

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.960
chose to use us because we
are, in reality, the largest

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.210
and most effective ticket
seller in the world.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.220
Even our competitors want
to come on our platform.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.070
- [Narrator] To which Taylor
Swift's promoter AEG rebutted.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.590
- And they said, no, we
actually didn't have a choice.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.030
She's the biggest artist in the world.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.060
She's gonna tour in
stadiums, and those stadiums

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.850
are locked up in exclusive
contracts with Ticketmaster.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.680
- [Narrator] Live Nation
has said it didn't engage

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.720
in behavior that warranted litigation.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:49.553
The company added

00:05:49.553 --> 00:05:52.170
that the ticketing industry
is more competitive than ever.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.670
- It's pointed to some

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:58.140
of the deals that it's lost in
venues to competing services

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.480
and it says that it continues to innovate

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.280
and make its technology better.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.920
- [Narrator] The Justice
Department declined to comment.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:07.380
While calls build for
the DOJ to undo the deal,

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.850
many industry experts wonder

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.100
if reversing the merge
would solve the issue.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.130
- Ticketmaster would still
be the dominant player

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.720
in the ticketing industry.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.380
It would still control some
70 to 80% of the market

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.810
and it would still have these
exclusive ticketing contracts

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:22.950
with the venues.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.110
- [Narrator] But undoing the
merge isn't the only option

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.640
at the DOJ's disposal.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.070
- They can also do investigations

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:31.380
into whether current business practices

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.660
are unlawful under separate laws.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:34.770
- [Narrator] The legal actions taken

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:37.200
against Ticketmaster and
Live Nation are pending.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.033
In the meantime,

00:06:38.033 --> 00:06:40.470
Taylor Swift fans are
scavenging resale sites,

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.532
still hoping to secure tickets.

00:06:42.532 --> 00:06:45.199
(pensive music)

